  renv is the right home. Reasons:

  Why renv for these three R packages

  1. Version pinning is the entire point. renv.lock records exact versions and
  source; a Dockerfile install.packages() grabs whatever is latest at build time
  unless you hand-pin versions — which just reimplements renv, badly. The
  compendium's reproducibility guarantee lives in renv.lock.
  2. One source of truth across host and container. renv.lock drives the
  container build and any host renv::restore() (and your make r host fallback).
  Install them only in the Dockerfile and the host environment silently lacks
  them, so the two diverge.
  3. renv stays consistent. A Dockerfile-only install is invisible to renv:
  renv::status() shows nothing, and a later renv::restore() could prune or
  conflict with it. Declaring in DESCRIPTION + renv::snapshot() keeps status
  clean.
  4. It eliminates the writability problem. If they're baked into the renv
  library at build time, the render step needs no install at all — the read-only
  /opt/renv/library error and my install-if-missing patch both become moot. The
  render just finds them on .libPaths().

  Mechanically: add to DESCRIPTION (these are Suggests for a compendium that
  renders), renv::snapshot() to capture them into renv.lock, rebuild. The
  Dockerfile's existing renv::restore() bakes them.

  Why the Dockerfile still matters — for the OS layer

  renv manages R packages, not system libraries. These belong in the Dockerfile
  via apt-get:

  - pandoc — already correct in your Dockerfile (line 46).
