❯ what is the idea for a 'dummy report'? One that uses all latex packages zzc   
  users are likely to call?                                                     

⏺ Right — that's exactly the idea, and your "packages users are likely to call"
  instinct is the crux of it. Let me lay out the concept and the design choice.

  The mechanism

  At build time, as root, render a throwaway .Rmd to PDF inside the Dockerfile.
  That render makes tinytex/tlmgr download and install every .sty the document
  pulls in — and because it runs as root, there's no permission problem. Those
  packages get baked into the image's TeX tree. Then at real render time (as
  non-root analyst), the packages are already present, so nothing needs
  installing — the failure mode disappears entirely. It converts an unreliable,
  networked, permission-sensitive runtime step into a deterministic build step.

  What goes in the dummy — two philosophies

  (a) Mirror the actual template (deterministic). Render a copy of zzcollab's
  own templates/report.Rmd — same bookdown::pdf_document2, same header-includes
  (setspace), a kable table, a figure, a [@marwick2017] citation + CSL. This
  installs exactly the LaTeX closure the default report needs — no guessing, and
  it stays correct as long as the dummy tracks the template. This is the robust
  core.

  (b) Kitchen-sink of likely-used features (superset). Add the LaTeX that zzc
  users commonly reach for but the bare template doesn't use: wide/long tables
  (booktabs, longtable, multirow, kableExtra's deps), float/figure placement,
  caption, maybe amsmath/amssymb math, xcolor, hyperref. This pre-installs a
  broader set so the first time a user adds a table or a theorem, it already
  works.

  My recommendation

  Do both, layered: a dummy that renders the real template (a) plus a short
  "common extensions" section (b) — but accept it can't be exhaustive. The
  honest limit of pre-baking is that it can only cover what you anticipate; a
  user who adds tikz or some niche package will still need an install. So pair
  the dummy with strategy 1 (add analyst to staff) so the long tail still
  auto-installs at render time. Baked set = fast + reproducible for the common
  case; staff membership = flexible for the rest.

  Two caveats worth designing for:
  - Reproducibility: baking pins packages to build time, but tlmgr's repo moves;
  without pinning the repo (strategy 5), an old image's runtime installs break
  after ~1 year (rocker-versioned2 #349). Baking the common set reduces how
  often runtime install is even needed.
  - Build cost: the first dummy render downloads a chunk of TeX Live (minutes,
  one-time per image build), which is the price for fast, reliable renders
  afterward.

  This belongs in the zzcollab publishing-profile Dockerfile generation, so
  every verse project inherits it — not just peng1.

