  BuildKit falls back to --progress=plain, which is the raw log dump you're
  seeing. So step one is often just: stop piping, let BuildKit render its own
  progress.

  Three implementation levels

  1. Cleanest minimal — gum spin + saved log (fits zzcollab's stack). zzcollab
  already uses gum. Run the build to a log file behind a spinner, then offer to
  view it:
  local log=".zzcollab/docker-build.log"
  if gum spin --title "Building image (a few minutes)…" -- \
        sh -c "DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 docker build -t \"$img\" . >\"$log\" 2>&1";
  then
    log_success "Image built.  Full log: $log"
    gum_confirm "View the build log?" && ${PAGER:-less} "$log"
  else
    log_error "Build failed — last lines:"; tail -n 30 "$log"
    log_info "Full log: $log"
  fi
  No log spam on success; the log is always saved; opt-in to view; on failure
  you auto-see the error tail. This is the smallest change and matches your
  "progress + option to list the log" ask.

  2. Real step progress bar. Drive a bar from BuildKit's step completions: count
  Dockerfile instructions for the total, run --progress=plain into a tee that
  both saves the log and watches for #N … DONE lines to advance a gum/printf
  bar. More faithful, but BuildKit's internal step numbering doesn't map 1:1 to
  Dockerfile lines, so the bar is approximate and the parser is more fragile.

  3. Just use BuildKit's native UI. Don't capture output at all —
  DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 docker build to the terminal gives the collapsing per-step
  progress for free, and separately --progress=plain to a log file if you also
  want the saved copy. Least code, but you don't control the look and can't
  easily add a "view log?" prompt after.

