❯ will this latex related build level be cached?                                

⏺ Sharp question — and the answer differs between local and CI, which matters a
  lot here.

  Locally: yes, with a placement caveat

  Docker caches each instruction's layer. The warm-up RUN text is static, so
  once it runs, rebuilds reuse the cached layer instantly — but cache 
  invalidates from the first changed layer downward. If the warm-up sits after
  COPY renv.lock + renv::restore, then every time renv.lock changes, the renv
  layer changes and invalidates the warm-up layer, re-running the whole LaTeX
  install. So I'll place the warm-up early — before the renv.lock COPY — so
  lockfile churn doesn't blow it away. The base image and the LaTeX layer are
  the stable foundation; renv is the volatile top.

  In CI: no, not by default — and this is the real cost

  GitHub Actions runners are ephemeral: each render job starts with an empty
  Docker cache. The render workflow does a fresh docker build every run, so the 
  warm-up RUN re-executes on every push that touches analysis/, re-installing
  the entire LaTeX closure (several minutes each time, even native). Layer
  caching does nothing across runs unless you give BuildKit a persistent cache
  backend.

  Two ways to fix that:

  1. BuildKit GHA cache (quick). The render job already uses
  docker/setup-buildx-action, so add --cache-from type=gha --cache-to type=gha
  (or switch to docker/build-push-action with those cache fields). BuildKit then
  persists layers in GitHub's cache between runs, and the warm-up layer is
  restored instead of rebuilt.
  2. Build once, push to a registry, pull in CI (durable, zzcollab-aligned).
  Build the verse image with the warm-up baked, push it to GHCR, and have the
  render job docker pull it instead of docker build. The LaTeX cost is paid once
  at image-publish time, not per render. This matches zzcollab's existing "team
  image" model (make docker-push-team).

  Recommendation: for the immediate fix, add the GHA cache to the render job
  (small workflow change). Longer term, the pull-a-prebuilt-image path is the
  right architecture — it's also faster and more reproducible than rebuilding
  every time.

