⏺ Here is each in plain language, with what problem it solves.

  LaTeX warm-up at build time

  To turn a report into a PDF, the system uses LaTeX, a typesetting engine.
  LaTeX needs many small add-on files (for math symbols, tables, and so on). The
  rocker/verse image ships only a bare LaTeX, and it would normally fetch the
  missing pieces the first time you render, but it could not do that as the
  ordinary (non-administrator) user the workflow runs as, so the PDF failed.

  The 'warm-up' fixes this by rendering two throwaway documents while the Docker
  image is being built (when we do have administrator rights). One mirrors a
  real report; the other deliberately uses a broad set of LaTeX features
  statisticians commonly need (tables, math, plots). Rendering them forces LaTeX
  to download and install all those add-ons once, and they get 'baked'
  permanently into the image. After that, real reports render quickly with
  nothing left to fetch.

  Self-adapting PDF/HTML render

  The same report-rendering workflow ships with every project, but only some
  projects have LaTeX installed (the publishing setup does; the lighter ones do
  not). Instead of forcing one format, the workflow now checks at run time
  whether a LaTeX engine is present: if yes, it produces a PDF; if no, it
  produces an HTML page. So a publishing project gets a manuscript PDF, and a
  minimal project still works (HTML) without needing LaTeX. One file, doing the
  right thing automatically in each environment.

  CSL scaffolded + example citation

  A CSL file is a 'citation style' definition. It tells the document how to
  format references (which journal's style: author-date, numbered, and so on).
  The starter report pointed at a specific CSL file, but the project setup never
  actually copied that file into the new project, so rendering failed looking
  for a file that was not there. 'Scaffolded' means the setup now copies the CSL
  file into the project automatically. Separately, the starter report listed
  one reference in its bibliography but never cited it, so the 'References'
  section came out empty. Adding an 'example citation' means the introduction
  now actually cites that reference, so a freshly created project demonstrates a
  complete, working citation-to-reference chain out of the box.

  renv activate.R recursion guard

  renv is the R tool that pins exact package versions. It uses a small startup
  script, activate.R, that runs every time R starts in the project. The version
  the setup wrote was too naive: when renv installed a package, that
  installation launches its own little R process, which re-ran the same startup
  script, which tried to install renv again, which launched another process, and
  so on. It looped forever until the job ran out of memory and was killed. The
  'guard' is a check, copied from renv's own proper startup script, that detects
  'I am running inside one of renv's helper processes' and quietly does nothing
  in that case. That breaks the loop, so the package check completes normally.

  Add the GHA cache (GHA = GitHub Actions)

  Every time the report workflow runs on GitHub, it builds the Docker image from
  scratch on a fresh, empty machine, which means it re-does the slow LaTeX
  warm-up every single time (about eight minutes per run). 'Adding the GHA
  cache' means telling the build system to save the finished image layers in
  GitHub's storage and reuse them on the next run, instead of rebuilding. The
  first run still pays the eight minutes; later runs restore the warm-up layer
  in seconds. The alternative is to build the image once, publish it to a
  registry, and have the workflow download it rather than build it at all.
  Either way, you stop paying the LaTeX-install cost on every push. This is the
  one item we have not done yet.

