⏺ Not bad — it's the more reproducible choice, and it is what
  reproducible-research practice actually recommends. But it has two real costs,
  and on the verse profile specifically there is one trap you must avoid. Let
  me be concrete.

  The costs

  1. Redundancy, and the drift it can cause. The verse image already contains
  dplyr, ggplot2, rmarkdown, and their closure (~100+ packages once you include
  transitive deps). If renv.lock also lists them, the same packages are pinned
  in two places: the image (by its dated PPM snapshot) and renv.lock (by its
  recorded versions). Redundant storage is harmless (renv.lock is just text).
  The real risk is drift: if renv.lock says dplyr 1.1.4 but the image baked
  dplyr 1.1.5, the lockfile now lies about the environment. A manifest that
  disagrees with the actual image is worse than no manifest, because it gives
  false confidence.

  2. Maintenance. A complete renv.lock has to be kept complete. Every time you
  add a package to your analysis, you must renv::snapshot() to update it, and on
  this setup that means a round-trip into the container to snapshot. That is
  the normal renv workflow, but it is friction compared to today's "the image
  just has everything" convenience.

  The trap, and how to avoid it

  The drift risk is entirely avoidable: generate renv.lock by snapshotting 
  inside the verse container itself. If the lock is produced from the image's
  actual installed packages, then by construction renv.lock == what the image 
  provides — they cannot disagree. The redundancy becomes benign (two consistent
  copies), check-renv passes honestly, and renv.lock is a faithful, restorable
  manifest. The cost is just the snapshot step on each dependency change.

  What you must not do is hand-maintain a complete renv.lock separately from the
  image, or pin versions in renv.lock that you hope match the image. That is
  where it goes bad.

  The honest tension

  A complete renv.lock and a baked-base image are doing the same job twice. The
  purest "renv.lock is the manifest" world uses a minimal base (rocker/r-ver)
  and lets renv.lock drive everything: the image is literally
  restore(renv.lock), so there is exactly one source of truth and zero drift.
  The price is slower builds (you compile/install the whole stack via renv
  instead of getting it pre-baked from verse).

  So it is really a profile choice:

  - verse + snapshot-to-lock: fast builds (baked), renv.lock generated from the
  image so it stays consistent, benign redundancy, snapshot maintenance. Good if
  you want verse's speed and a faithful manifest.
  - minimal + full renv.lock: one source of truth, no redundancy, maximal
  reproducibility, but slower builds and you give up verse's pre-baked
  convenience. Purest.

  Bottom line: making renv.lock the complete manifest is not bad and is more
  reproducible. The only way it becomes bad is if the lock drifts from the
  image, which you prevent by generating the lock from the image rather than
  maintaining it by hand. If reproducibility is the priority and you can accept
  the snapshot round-trip, it is the right move; if you want zero
  double-bookkeeping, that argues for a minimal base instead of verse.

