⏺ Good foundational question — and it matters for what we're building, since
  check_packages enforces both. They aren't duplicates; they record different 
  things for different consumers.

  DESCRIPTION — the abstract contract

  DESCRIPTION declares what the project directly depends on, by role
  (Imports/Suggests/Depends), with optional version floors (dplyr (>= 1.1.0)).
  It is:

  - direct dependencies only — you list dplyr, not its 20 transitive deps;
  - constraints, not exact versions — "at least 1.1.0", or no version at all;
  - human-authored intent, grouped by purpose (runtime vs test vs build);
  - what the R package machinery reads: R CMD check,
  install.packages(dependencies=TRUE), pak, CRAN. Because your compendium is an
  R package, this is what makes it a valid, checkable, installable package.

  renv.lock — the exact reproducible snapshot

  renv.lock pins the exact versions (plus source and hash) of every package in
  the environment, including the full transitive closure. It is:

  - the complete dependency tree — dplyr and vctrs 0.6.5, rlang 1.1.4, cli 
  3.6.3, …;
  - exact pins, not floors — the precise version that reproduces the result;
  - machine-generated by renv::snapshot(), not hand-edited;
  - what renv::restore() reads to rebuild the environment byte-for-version
  identically.

  Why both, not one

  ┌────────────┬────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐
  │            │        DESCRIPTION         │          renv.lock           │
  ├────────────┼────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Records    │ direct deps, roles,        │ full closure, exact          │
  │            │ version floors             │ versions, hashes             │
  ├────────────┼────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Authored   │ human (intent)             │ renv (snapshot)              │
  │ by         │                            │                              │
  ├────────────┼────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Read by    │ R CMD check, installers,   │ renv::restore                │
  │            │ CRAN                       │                              │
  ├────────────┼────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Answers    │ "what does this need?"     │ "exactly what reproduces     │
  │            │                            │ it?"                         │
  └────────────┴────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘

  They hold non-overlapping information. You could not derive the exact-pinned
  closure from DESCRIPTION (it only has floors and direct deps), and you would
  not want to hand-maintain the closure in DESCRIPTION (that is renv's job).
  Conversely, renv.lock has no notion of roles (is tinytest a test-only dep?) or
  of being a declarable package contract — that is DESCRIPTION's job.

  A package in renv.lock but not DESCRIPTION is pinned but undeclared: R CMD 
  check doesn't know it's needed, and it reads as an undocumented dependency. A
  package in DESCRIPTION but not renv.lock is declared but unpinned:
  renv::restore() won't install it, so a collaborator's environment is
  incomplete. Your compendium is both an R package and a reproducible project,
  so it needs both: DESCRIPTION for the package-ness, renv.lock for the exact
  reproduction. That is exactly why check_packages flags a code dependency
  missing from either.

  So the "redundancy" you're sensing is only at the surface (a direct dep's name
  appears in both); underneath, each file carries information the other cannot.

