  The precise invariant

  There's only one kind of real version mismatch here: a DESCRIPTION floor that 
  the renv.lock pin fails to satisfy (e.g. DESCRIPTION dplyr (>= 2.0), renv.lock
  dplyr 1.1.4). A floor-less DESCRIPTION entry plus a lock pin is normal, not a
  conflict. So the checkable rule is clean and mechanical:

  ▎ for every DESCRIPTION dependency that carries a floor, the renv.lock pin 
  ▎ must satisfy it (lock_version >= floor).

  The correct action

  Detect and report — do not silently auto-fix. A floor is a deliberate human
  statement: "my code needs at least version X." If the lock pins below X, the
  reproducible environment does not meet the declared requirement. That is a
  genuine defect the developer must see, not paper over.

  Is it fixable?

  - Detectable: yes, cleanly and mechanically. (zzrenvcheck doesn't do this
  today — it's name-only — so it would be a real enhancement.)
  - Auto-fixable in one direction: no, not safely, because the fix depends on
  which file is authoritative, and that's intent the tool can't read. There are
  two reconciliations, pointing opposite ways:

  - (a) Bring the environment up to the declaration — renv::install("dplyr") to
  a version satisfying the floor, then renv::snapshot(). This respects the
  stated requirement and is the principled fix: the floor says you need ≥2.0, so
  the environment should provide ≥2.0.

  - (b) Relax the declaration to the lock — lower or drop the floor to match
  what's pinned. This is "fixing the test instead of the bug" — legitimate only
  if you confirm the floor was spurious (e.g. over-cautious or auto-guessed).

  So the right tool behavior is: flag it, default to recommending (a), and offer
  to perform (a) semi-automatically (it's the safe direction — you can always
  install a satisfying version and re-snapshot). It should not auto-apply (b),
  because silently weakening a declared minimum hides a real requirement and
  defeats the point of the floor.

  The one nuance in our favor: since auto_fix never writes floors, the common
  path produces no conflicts at all. A conflict means a human deliberately
  raised a floor — exactly the case where you want a report and a decision, not
  an automatic rewrite.

