❯ what other R packages could be considered as 'dev tooling' and not a factor   
  in reproducibility?                                                           

⏺ Good question — it's the right way to think about what belongs in the
  manifest. The test is simple:

  ▎ Could this package's version change the analytical or rendered output? If 
  ▎ no, it's dev tooling and has no place in the reproducibility manifest 
  ▎ (renv.lock). Pin it at the platform layer (Dockerfile, dated PPM snapshot) 
  ▎ if you want a reproducible dev environment, but keep it out of renv.lock.

  There's a second, practical filter specific to renv that languageserver
  exemplifies:

  ▎ Is it referenced in the analysis code at all? renv only captures packages it
  ▎ sees via library()/require()/pkg::/DESCRIPTION. Packages invoked by the 
  ▎ editor, git hooks, or CI — never by the analysis code — are never captured, 
  ▎ so they can only end up in renv.lock if you force them there. Those are the 
  ▎ natural Dockerfile-only set.

  languageserver is special because it satisfies both (no output effect, never
  code-referenced). Here are others, grouped:

  Clearest dev tooling (like languageserver — usually not code-referenced, never
  affect output):
  - Editor / LSP / style: languageserver, lintr, styler (when run by the editor
  or pre-commit, not from a script)
  - Package development: devtools, usethis, roxygen2, pkgbuild, sessioninfo,
  remotes, pak
  - Docs / site: pkgdown
  - Profiling / debugging: profvis, bench, lobstr
  - Version control helpers: gert, gh, credentials, gitcreds
  - Build / check: rcmdcheck

  Honest borderline cases — decide deliberately, don't lump them in:
  - rmarkdown, knitr, bookdown, pandoc, tinytex/LaTeX — these render the report,
  so a version change can alter the produced artifact (page breaks, table
  formatting, citation rendering). They are reproducibility-relevant, not dev
  tooling. Keep them captured (which is why peng1 has knitr in Suggests and
  rmarkdown in the lock).
  - testthat, tinytest — test infrastructure. They don't produce the analysis
  output, but a test's pass/fail is part of reproducing your verification.
  Convention is to declare them in Suggests and let them be pinned; reasonable
  either way.
  - pkgload — borderline: it loads your package at render time (load_all), so it
  is code-referenced and does touch the run. Not pure tooling — which is why we
  kept it in the manifest (Suggests).
  - here — not tooling at all: it's analysis path resolution, code-referenced,
  output-relevant. Stays in the manifest.

  The practical upshot for zzcollab: the Dockerfile tools layer is the right
  home for the "clearest dev tooling" set above — installed via the dated PPM
  snapshot, never snapshotted into renv.lock. languageserver is the one zzcollab
  already installs there; if you later add lintr/styler for a pre-commit
  workflow, they belong in the same layer for the same reason. Everything that
  the analysis or its rendering touches belongs in renv.lock, captured by make 
  snapshot.

  One caveat worth stating: "doesn't affect reproducibility" means of the 
  results. If you care about a reproducible development environment
  (collaborators get the same devtools/roxygen2), pin those too — but in the
  Dockerfile, not the analysis manifest. Keeping the two layers separate is
  exactly what stops renv.lock from bloating back to the 115-package state we
  just escaped.

