  What zzcollab does now

  The code-quality feature installs a .pre-commit-config.yaml with the styler
  and lintr hooks (the lorenzwalthert/precommit hooks — --style_pkg=styler, id: 
  lintr). It does not install styler/lintr into the image; instead the user runs
  pre-commit install and pre-commit run, and those hooks self-manage their own
  R packages in an isolated pre-commit environment.

  The problem for your design

  Those R hooks need R to run them, and pre-commit runs on the host at commit
  time. With no R on the host, the host pre-commit R hooks can't run. So the
  current code-quality feature quietly assumes host R — exactly the assumption
  your design rejects. Today, styler/lintr are in neither the image nor
  (reliably) runnable, for a no-host-R user.

  So: yes, install them — but conditionally and in the container

  For a no-host-R-consistent code-quality workflow, the linters have to run in 
  the container, which means they must be in the image. The clean shape mirrors
  the languageserver toggle:

  - Install styler/lintr in the Dockerfile, gated on the code-quality feature
  (CONFIG_FEAT_CODE_QUALITY). Not unconditional — a project without code-quality
  shouldn't pay for them; not in renv.lock — they don't touch the result.
  - Run them in the container, not via host pre-commit: either a make lint /
  make style target that runs styler/lintr in the container, or configure
  pre-commit to docker exec into the container (the same bridge pattern as your
  case-3 LSP). That way the linters see the project's actual R and library.

  So the rule generalizes: a dev tool goes in the image only when the feature 
  that uses it is enabled, and it goes in the image rather than the host because
  there is no host R to run it on. languageserver is gated on the LSP/editing
  choice; styler/lintr are gated on code-quality. Both Dockerfile, both
  conditional, neither in renv.lock.

