  The trim exposed a real render bug — now fixed

  Diagnosis (verified in a verse container, not inferred):
  The render set .libPaths() from Sys.glob("/opt/renv/library/*/*"), which
  resolves to .../R-4.6 — one level above the actual package directory
  .../R-4.6/x86_64-pc-linux-gnu. The glob was always wrong; it only worked
  because here/yaml were also in the site-library via the explicit
  install.packages. Once the trim moved them to manifest-only, here existed
  solely in the renv library, the shallow glob missed it, and the render died
  with there is no package called 'here'.

  Where each mechanism lands packages (all confirmed in-container):

  ┌────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┐
  │             mechanism              │            destination             │
  ├────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ install.packages                   │ renv::paths$library() =            │
  │ (RENV_PATHS_LIBRARY set)           │ .../R-4.6/<arch>                   │
  ├────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ renv::restore                      │ same                               │
  ├────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ renv::hydrate                      │ same (dplyr found there, loadable) │
  └────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────┘

  So renv::paths[['library']]() is the single authoritative answer for restore,
  hydrate, and install alike.

  Fix (3 files): replaced the shallow glob with renv::paths[['library']]() and a
  correct-depth */*/* fallback, in peng1's render-report.yml (pushed, 8783c77)
  and zzcollab's templates/workflows/render-report.yml +
  templates/gitlab/.gitlab-ci.yml (committed, 000ac94). R Package Check was
