  Conclusion: no package is the culprit — the architecture is

  Your hypothesis isn't supported by the data. Look at the size-vs-time
  correlation — there is none:

  - The biggest downloads install instantly. amsfonts (3.5 MB), pgf (702k),
  pgfplots (519k) — all fetch in under a second. pgf landed at 294.4s, just 0.5s
  after fp at 293.9s, because it came in as a dependency in the same tlmgr
  call.
  - Notice the back-to-back pairs with +0.5s deltas (tabu→varwidth,
  environ→trimspaces, fp→pgf): when one tlmgr call installs several packages,
  they're all fast.
  - The ~14s per package is the pdflatex recompile that tinytex runs between
  each discovery. It renders the doc, hits one missing .sty, installs just that
  one, and recompiles the whole document again. 24 packages × ~14s ≈ 5.4 min.
  - The single ~39s "outlier" (#17→#18) wasn't a heavy package — mathtools is
  21k. That window contained a one-time tlmgr update --all --self self-update
  pass (~25s).

  The real fix

  The bottleneck is tinytex's install-one-recompile-loop, driven by rendering a
  document and letting it discover packages lazily. Replace that with a single 
  bulk tlmgr install of the known list, then render once:

  RUN R -e "tinytex::tlmgr_install(c('amsfonts','booktabs','setspace', \
    'multirow','wrapfig','float','colortbl','pdflscape','tabu','varwidth', \
    'threeparttable','threeparttablex','environ','trimspaces','ulem', \
    'makecell','mathtools','fancyhdr','caption','enumitem','fp','pgf', \
    'pgfplots','siunitx'))"

  One tlmgr call installs all 24 in a single pass (~20–30s of actual download),
  and one final render verifies. That collapses the ~5.4 min recompile loop to
  well under a minute even on a cold build — before any image caching. Disabling
  the tlmgr update --all --self step saves the extra ~25s on top.

