❯ wait why not keep /$UBUNTU_CODENAME and PPM_SNAPSHOT generic to futureproof   

⏺ Good challenge. The honest reason I hardcoded is a constraint, not a
  preference: .Rprofile is R code, and R does not expand shell variables. The
  literal text $UBUNTU_CODENAME in a quoted R string stays the seven characters
  $, U, B... it is not a variable reference. So "keeping it generic" as-written
  is exactly the broken state we started from. Genericness needs a resolution 
  mechanism, not just the placeholder tokens.

  The template's placeholders were meant to be substituted at scaffold time (by
  sed/envsubst), and fisher is proof that silently fails. So there are two ways
  to make it genuinely future-proof, and I agree hardcoding is the weaker one:

  The clean future-proof design: resolve from the environment at runtime. The
  Dockerfile already sets RENV_CONFIG_REPOS_OVERRIDE to the full PPM URL as a
  single source of truth. Have .Rprofile derive repos from that same env var
  instead of embedding any URL:

  # container branch: derive PPM repo from the env the Dockerfile already sets
  ppm <- Sys.getenv("RENV_CONFIG_REPOS_OVERRIDE",

  "https://packagemanager.posit.co/cran/__linux__/noble/latest")
  options(repos = c(CRAN = ppm), renv.repos.cran = ppm)

  Now the codename and snapshot live in exactly one place (the Dockerfile ENV),
  .Rprofile follows automatically, and there is no scaffold-substitution step
  that can silently fail. Bump the snapshot by editing one Dockerfile line. This
  eliminates the entire bug class rather than patching this instance.

  This is better than what I did. Two scope questions before I change it:

  1. Apply the env-resolution rewrite to fisher's .Rprofile? (Replaces the
  noble/2026-06-29 literal I just wrote with the Sys.getenv form.)
  2. Also fix it in the zzcollab template .Rprofile? The
  placeholder-substitution defect is a template bug that will bite every future
  scaffold the same way. Fixing it upstream is the real remedy; fisher-only
  leaves the template broken. My recommendation is to do both.

  Which scope do you want?

