❯ how are you going to reset renv.lock?                                         

⏺ Good question to press on, because thinking it through carefully, a blind rm 
  renv.lock is the wrong mechanism, and my playbook's "bare it" wording
  oversimplifies. There are two real options and they differ in risk.

  The subtlety

  renv::snapshot() can only lock packages that are actually installed in the
  image. So whatever "reset" means, every code-used package must end up
  installed before the snapshot, or it silently drops from the lock. That
  interacts with two things:

  - The Dockerfile does COPY renv.lock then renv::restore(). If I delete the
  lock before building, the COPY fails; if I bare it, restore installs nothing,
  and the regrow then depends entirely on what the verse base provides plus what
  I explicitly install. That is exactly the verse-contents probe I flagged
  earlier and never ran. Bare-resetting without it risks a build that drops
  kableExtra (and others) from the lock.
  - Version coherence: a bare reset reinstalls everything at PPM-2026-06-29
  versions; keeping the lock restores the current (older) versions.

  Option A (recommended): let snapshot rewrite the lock, do not bare it

  1. Build the verse image with the current lock. restore() populates the
  project library, so kableExtra and the report deps are guaranteed present.
  2. Run make snapshot in the container: renv::hydrate() + renv::snapshot(type =
  "implicit") + check_packages.

  Implicit snapshot rewrites the lock to exactly the packages the code uses plus
  their recursive deps. That prunes the cruft (Exact, which appears only in
  LaTeX prose so renv does not detect it) and adds the used-but-unlocked ones
  that are now installed — critically Rcpp, which the verse base provides and
  R/RcppExports.R uses, so it gets hydrated in and locked, fixing the gap. No
  verse-contents probe needed, because anything already in the lock is restored,
  and the one missing item (Rcpp) is definitely in verse.

  Tradeoff: restored packages keep their current locked versions rather than
  jumping to PPM-2026-06-29. That is fine for the gate: zzrenvcheck v0.4.0
  checks that DESCRIPTION and renv.lock agree with each other, not that they
  match the newest PPM snapshot.

  Option B: hard bare-reset

  Bare the lock, build so nothing restores, reinstall the used set fresh from
  PPM-2026-06-29, snapshot. Gives uniform current versions, but requires the
  verse-contents probe first (to know the explicit-install list), needs a
  Dockerfile tweak so the build succeeds with no lock, and risks a broken render
  if any needed package is neither in verse nor installed. More work, more
  risk, for a version-uniformity that the gate does not require.

  My recommendation

  Option A. It achieves the actual goals of the "reset" — drop the dead Exact
  entry, add the missing Rcpp, produce a minimal lock that passes the gate —
  with far less risk and no dependency on the unrun probe. I would update the
  playbook's Phase 2 wording accordingly (prefer snapshot-rewrite; bare-reset
  only when you deliberately want a version bump and have confirmed the base
  contents).

  Want me to go with Option A?

