❯ should we add a flag to zzc that resets renv.lock?                            

⏺ Short answer: no, I don't think a dedicated "reset renv.lock" flag earns its
  place, and adding one would mostly create a footgun. Here is the reasoning.

  The safe reset already exists: it is make snapshot

  What we called Option A is make snapshot. renv::snapshot(type = "implicit")
  rewrites the lock to exactly the used set plus recursive deps, which means it
  already prunes unused entries (Exact) and adds used-but-unlocked ones that are
  installed (Rcpp). So the common, safe "reset" is not a missing capability; it
  is the existing target run against the new image. A zzc reset-lock flag would
  just be an alias for something we ship.

  The only thing a real "reset" flag would add is the dangerous part

  The one behavior make snapshot does not do is the hard bare-and-reinstall that
  bumps every package to the current PPM versions (Option B). That is:

  - rare — you want it only when you deliberately change the base image or PPM
  snapshot and want versions to follow, and
  - destructive — it throws away the pinned versions that are the whole point of
  a lockfile, and it can silently drop a package that is neither in the base
  nor explicitly reinstalled.

  Wrapping that in a one-word zzc --reset-lock invites people to nuke a good
  lockfile expecting a tidy-up and instead get a version bump plus possible
  dropouts. Destructive operations should be verbose and deliberate, not a
  convenient flag.

  If we want to support the version-bump case, name it honestly and guard it

  The legitimate need behind your question is "refresh the lock to current
  versions after changing the environment." That is renv::update() semantics,
  not "reset." So if anything, add a clearly-labeled make refresh target that:

  1. copies renv.lock to renv.lock.bak first,
  2. runs renv::update() (or bare + reinstall) against the current repos,
  3. renv::snapshot() + check_packages,
  4. prints a diff of what versions changed.

  That gives the capability with a name that says what it does and a backup that
  undoes a mistake. Calling it "reset" hides the version-bump behind a word
  that sounds safe.

  For the fisher migration specifically

  Nothing new is needed. make snapshot on the verse image is the reset. The gap
  is discoverability, not capability, so the right fix is one line in the
  playbook Phase 2: "the reset mechanism is make snapshot against the migrated
  image; a hard bare-reset is only for a deliberate version bump." I already
  flagged updating that wording.

  So: I would not add a reset flag. I would (optionally, later) add a
  guardrailed make refresh for deliberate version bumps, and update the playbook
  to name make snapshot as the reset. Want me to do that playbook wording fix,
