  Answer: check_packages is a host/static operation

  By inspection of the source, check_packages only ever: parses code files,
  DESCRIPTION, and renv.lock (all on the host filesystem), and queries
  available.packages() for versions. It never loads or requires the target
  packages to be installed — it validates declaration, not installation. So
  there is no reason it must run in the container. The container is only needed
  for renv::hydrate()/renv::snapshot(), which do operate on the installed
  library.

  Your architecture already exists

  The make r target does exactly what you're describing (Makefile:177–192): it
  runs the interactive R session in the container, and then, after the container
  exits, on the host, it runs zzrenvcheck::check_packages(auto_fix = TRUE, 
  strict = TRUE) — installing zzrenvcheck on the host first if it's missing. So
  zzrenvcheck is already treated as a host tool. The bug is simply that snapshot
  / check-renv / reset-lock were written inconsistently to run it in-container
  via DOCKER_RUN.

  So the clean fix is to make them match make r:
  - snapshot: renv::hydrate() + renv::snapshot() in the container, then
  check_packages() on the host.
  - check-renv (and -no-fix/-no-strict): host only — no container at all, since
  it's pure static analysis.
  - reset-lock: host check_packages(fresh = TRUE).

  That deletes the whole "zzrenvcheck-in-container" problem: no baking, no
  docker commit, no ephemeral reinstall, no phantom target.

  Two refinements to your proposal

  1. Prefer a Makefile prerequisite over .Rprofile for the install. Putting the
  zzrenvcheck install in .Rprofile means every host R session — including plain
  interactive work — checks/installs it, which is heavier and more surprising
  than doing it only when a validation target runs. The make r block (lines
  179–188) already installs-if-missing at the point of use; I'd model the other
  targets on that (extracted into one ensure-zzrenvcheck host prereq). If you
  specifically want zero-friction on first make snapshot, the prereq gives that
  without touching interactive sessions.
  2. Version coherence needs the pinned repo on the host. This is the one real
  caveat. On the host, .Rprofile sets repos to cloud.r-project.org (live
  latest), not the pinned noble/2026-06-29 PPM snapshot the image was built
  from. So when auto_fix adds a package, or fresh re-resolves everything, the
  host would pull newer versions than the image, drifting renv.lock away from
  what's installed. The existing make r check quietly accepts this; for
  correctness I'd have the new targets set options(repos = <PPM snapshot>)
  before calling check_packages, so host-resolved versions match the image. (The
  snapshot happy-path rarely adds, so it mostly bites reset-lock/fresh.)

