  The key insight

  Both false positives are LaTeX prose, not code. Exact::exact.test and
  remotes::install_github(...) appear inside \texttt{...} in the manuscript text
  (comparisons to another package; install instructions), never in an R code
  chunk. zzrenvcheck's scanner greps the whole .Rmd, including prose, so any
  pkg:: in \texttt{} gets counted.

  That reframes the cleanest fix: **teach zzrenvcheck to scan only fenced R code
  chunks (```{r}) in .Rmd/.qmd, not the surrounding markdown/LaTeX prose.**
  Real package usage in an .Rmd is always in a code chunk; \texttt{pkg::fn} in
  prose is not. That single change eliminates *both* Exact and remotes without
  touching your manuscript text — and it's a general correctness win for the
  framework, not a fisher hack.

  So the refined, precise fix plan:

  1. zzrenvcheck: chunk-aware .Rmd scanning (extract R code chunks first, then
  detect packages) — kills the Exact/remotes prose false positives.
