❯ what is the purpose of .renvignore?                                           

⏺ .renvignore is renv's mechanism to exclude files and directories from 
  dependency discovery — the scan that decides which packages get written into
  renv.lock.

  How it fits in renv's model

  renv keeps renv.lock in sync with your code by scanning the project
  (renv::dependencies()) for package references: library(pkg), require(pkg),
  pkg::fn, pacman::p_load(...), roxygen @import, etc. Every package it finds is
  treated as a project dependency and gets locked by renv::snapshot() (and
  staged by renv::hydrate()). .renvignore tells renv "don't scan these paths",
  so whatever packages they reference are left out of the lockfile.

  It uses gitignore-style syntax (bare names, globs, /-anchoring, ! negation,
  directory patterns), but it is a separate file from .gitignore and
  .Rbuildignore, which do unrelated jobs:

  ┌───────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │     file      │                         governs                          │
  ├───────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ .gitignore    │ what git tracks                                          │
  ├───────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ .Rbuildignore │ what's excluded from the built package tarball (R CMD    │
  │               │ build)                                                   │
  ├───────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ .renvignore   │ what renv scans for dependencies                         │
  └───────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  What it does and does not affect

  - Affects: which files renv reads to build the dependency set → therefore what
  snapshot/hydrate put in renv.lock.
  - Does not affect: package installation, restore, git, or whether a file
  renders/runs. It's purely about discovery, not execution.

  Why it matters here (fisher)

  paper.Rmd is a host-rendered submission manuscript whose setup chunk does
  pacman::p_load(skimr, janitor, tidyverse, lubridate, readxl, ...). renv's
  scanner does expand p_load, so without .renvignore it pulled that entire
  tidyverse closure into renv.lock — the 75→156 bloat we saw. Listing paper.Rmd
  (and supplement.Rmd) in .renvignore makes renv skip those files, so their
  one-off manuscript packages never enter the sealed lock.

  The reason I extended zzrenvcheck to honor .renvignore (v0.6.0) is
  consistency: renv and the zzrenvcheck manifest gate now read the same
  exclusion list, so they can't disagree about which files count. Common uses
  beyond ours: example/vignette code with demo-only packages, vendored
  third-party sources, scratch/experimental scripts, and data-generation scripts
  that use tools you don't want to pin.

  Typical use: renv::dependencies() will actually show you what's being picked
  up (and from which files), which is how you decide what to add to .renvignore.

