❯ would we put the develop related packages in here like stylr, and             
  languageserver, etc.?                                                         

⏺ No — and the distinction matters. .renvignore excludes files, not packages.
  You can't list styler or languageserver in it; it takes file/directory
  patterns (like .gitignore), and it skips whatever those files reference.
  There's no package-name entry.

  So the real question is how dev tools stay out of renv.lock, and the answer is
  usually they never get in, automatically:

  Dev tools normally aren't discovered at all

  styler, lintr, languageserver, devtools, roxygen2 are invoked as tools, not
  called from your analysis or package code. You never write library(styler) in
  a report or styler:: in R/. You run them via make style / make lint, the IDE's
  LSP client, or devtools::check() at the console. Since renv's scanner only
  finds packages that are referenced in scanned files, these simply never appear
  in the dependency set → never enter renv.lock. No .renvignore needed.

  That's the same reasoning as the placement whitepaper: dev tooling lives in
  the Dockerfile (installed as an image layer), never in renv.lock, precisely
  because it supports the work rather than being part of it.

  The exception: a dev tool referenced in scanned code

  This is exactly the devtools case we hit.
  analysis/scripts/benchmark_manuscript_tables.R does devtools::load_all(), so
  renv/zzrenvcheck do see it. To exclude it you have three options, in rough
  order of preference:

  1. Don't reference it in scanned code — e.g. switch devtools::load_all() to
  pkgload::load_all() (lighter, and pkgload is a legitimate analysis dep), or
  library(fisherexacttestrx2) after make install.
  2. .renvignore the file — only if that whole script is dev-only/scratch and
  you're fine excluding all of its deps. Too blunt if the script also uses real
  analysis packages.
  3. renv's package-level ignore — this is the actual "ignore a package" knob:
  renv::settings$ignored.packages(c("devtools")), stored in renv/settings.json.
  That excludes the named package from snapshots regardless of which file
  references it. (zzrenvcheck does not read this yet — only .renvignore — so
  using it would reintroduce a scanner disagreement.)

  So: .renvignore is the wrong tool for "ignore styler." For genuine tools that
  aren't in your code, do nothing (they're already out). For the odd dev tool
  that is in your code, prefer fixing the reference (option 1);
  renv::settings$ignored.packages() is the package-level escape hatch if you
  can't.

