❯ as an edge case, what if someone did include 'styler' in the code             
  'library(styler)'?                                                            

⏺ If library(styler) sits in scanned code, then styler stops being "just
  tooling" and becomes a discovered dependency — and both scanners are right to
  treat it that way. Concretely:

  - renv finds it → make snapshot writes styler into renv.lock (and, since it's
  an analysis file, check_packages adds it to DESCRIPTION Suggests).
  - zzrenvcheck's gate finds it → until it's declared and locked, the manifest
  gate reports "used but not declared" and CI goes red; once snapshotted, it
  passes.

  The tools can't distinguish "a dev tool I happen to invoke" from "a package my
  code loads" — the signal is library(), and that's a genuine load. So the
  placement model doesn't actually break here; it resolves via the whitepaper's
  second test, "is it code-referenced?" Once the answer is yes, the package is
  no longer exempt.

  So the right response depends on why that line exists:

  1. It's genuinely part of the reproducible pipeline (e.g. a script that
  programmatically styles generated code as a real step). Then styler is a
  dependency and locking it is correct — the analysis won't reproduce without
  it. The "dev tooling → Dockerfile only" rule presumes the tool is invoked
  outside the analysis code (make targets, the IDE); once the code itself calls
  it, that presumption is gone.
  2. It's a stray or authoring convenience (someone loaded styler at the top of
  a report to tidy something while writing, but it's not part of the output).
  Then it's spurious, and in preference order:
    - Remove the library(styler) — cleanest; it doesn't belong in the analysis
  code.
    - .renvignore the file — only if that whole file is dev/scratch (too blunt
  if it also uses real packages).
    - renv::settings$ignored.packages("styler") — the package-level knob, but
  zzrenvcheck doesn't honor it, so the gate would still flag styler → the
  scanners disagree again. Avoid unless you also teach zzrenvcheck that setting.

  One consequence worth knowing: if styler ends up in renv.lock, renv::restore
  installs it into the project library, while the Dockerfile (if code-quality is
  on) also installs it into the site library. That's redundant, not broken —
  renv's project library wins at runtime, so the image's copy just goes unused.

  Bottom line: library(styler) in real code makes styler a real dependency, and
  the honest fix is to decide whether the code should load it. If yes, lock it.
  If no, delete the line — don't try to paper over a genuine code reference with
  an ignore rule.

