❯ does renv check to confirm that a package in code is installed in the R       
  library in the container?                                                     

⏺ Yes, but only at specific moments, not continuously — and it's worth
  separating the functions, because "check installed" and "check declared" are
  two different things done by two different tools.

  renv checks installation at snapshot and status time

  renv reconciles three things: the code's dependencies (renv::dependencies()),
  the installed project library, and renv.lock. The installation check happens
  in:

  - renv::snapshot() — before writing the lockfile, it validates that every
  package your code uses is actually installed in the active library. If a
  code-referenced package is missing, it reports "The following required 
  packages are not installed: ..." and, with validation on
  (renv.config.snapshot.validate, default TRUE), refuses to snapshot it. renv
  can only record a package it can see installed (it reads the installed
  version/source), so an uninstalled-but-used package is a hard stop, not a
  silent omission. This is the check that fired implicitly when make snapshot
  succeeded — it confirmed the used packages were present in /opt/renv/library.
  - renv::status() — the explicit reconciliation report. It lists "used in code
  but not installed," "installed but not recorded," "recorded but not
  installed," and version drift.

  What does not check installation:
  - renv::dependencies() — pure source parsing; never touches the library.
  - renv::restore() — installs from the lock into the library; it's the thing
  that makes packages installed, not a checker.
  - At runtime, a missing package isn't a renv check at all — library(pkg) just
  fails with base R's "there is no package called 'pkg'".

  In the container specifically

  Your Dockerfile sets ZZCOLLAB_AUTO_RESTORE=false ("the baked image library is
  authoritative"), so renv does not auto-restore or hard-validate on every R
  startup. renv's autoloader may print an "out of sync" hint, but it won't
  block. The real installation check only happens when you run renv::snapshot()
  / renv::status() (i.e. make snapshot).

  The key distinction from zzrenvcheck

  This is the part that trips people up:

  ┌─────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │          tool           │              question it answers              │
  ├─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ renv (snapshot/status)  │ is the code's package installed in the        │
  │                         │ library?                                      │
  ├─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ zzrenvcheck             │ is the code's package declared in DESCRIPTION │
  │ (check_packages)        │  and locked in renv.lock?                     │
  └─────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  zzrenvcheck is presence/declaration-only — it never checks whether anything is
  installed (which is exactly why we could move it to the host: it reads files,
  not a library). renv checks installation. They're complementary: renv
  guarantees the lock reflects what's really installed; zzrenvcheck guarantees
  the code, DESCRIPTION, and lock all agree. Neither alone covers both, which is
  why the pipeline runs renv's snapshot in the container and zzrenvcheck's gate
  on the host.

