❯ what is the purpose of the devtools→pkgload switch?                           

⏺ pkgload::load_all() does the identical job with a fraction of the dependency
  weight. The key fact: devtools::load_all() is pkgload::load_all() — devtools
  imports pkgload and re-exports it, so the function is literally the same code.
  The switch is behavior-preserving.

  The reasons:

  1. Dependency footprint. devtools is a heavy meta-package: requiring it pulls
  in its whole closure (usethis, remotes, pkgbuild, roxygen2, rcmdcheck,
  sessioninfo, testthat, …) — dozens of packages. pkgload is small. The
  benchmark script only needs load_all, not any of the rest.
  2. Manifest leanness / reproducibility. Because the script calls
  devtools::load_all, devtools becomes a discovered dependency (the
  "code-referenced" test), so it and its entire closure would have to be in
  renv.lock + DESCRIPTION and restored into the image. That's a lot of
  dev-tooling baggage pinned for one convenience call. pkgload replaces it with
  a single light, legitimate analysis helper.
  3. Placement alignment. Per the placement whitepaper, devtools is dev tooling
  that belongs in the Dockerfile, not the reproducibility manifest — but a
  devtools:: call in scanned code forces it into the manifest anyway. Switching
  to pkgload removes that leak: the script keeps working, and the manifest stays
  lean.

