❯ do all 'downstream' dependencies of Imports packages also get listed in       
  Imports?                                                                      

⏺ No — and this is the key to why renv.lock (57) is so much bigger than
  DESCRIPTION (8).

  DESCRIPTION lists only direct dependencies

  In Imports (and Suggests, Depends, LinkingTo) you list only the packages your 
  own code directly uses — the ones you library(), ::, or LinkingTo. You do not
  list the downstream/transitive dependencies. They're pulled in automatically:
  when R installs your package, it reads your Imports, installs each, and then
  recursively installs their Imports (and Depends/LinkingTo), all the way down.
  Each package declares its own direct deps; the chain assembles itself.

  So if you Imports: ggplot2, you write just ggplot2 — not rlang, glue, scales,
  gtable, etc. Those are ggplot2's problem to declare, and they arrive
  automatically.

  One nuance on which edges recurse:
  - Imports / Depends / LinkingTo recurse — installing a package installs these
  for the whole tree.
  - Suggests does not recurse — a dependency's Suggests are not installed
  automatically (they're optional). Only your top-level Suggests are considered,
  and even then only when you ask for them.

  The full closure lives in renv.lock

  renv.lock is the opposite: it records the complete transitive closure — every
  direct dependency plus all downstream deps — each pinned to an exact version.
  That's the whole point of the lockfile: exact, complete reproducibility.

  That's exactly fisher's numbers:

  ┌─────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────┐
  │    file     │                      contents                      │ count │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────┤
  │ DESCRIPTION │ direct deps only (Rcpp + the 7 analysis/test       │ 8     │
  │             │ packages)                                          │       │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────┤
  │ renv.lock   │ those 8 plus their entire downstream closure,      │ 57    │
  │             │ pinned                                             │       │
  └─────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────┘

  The 57 = the closure of the 8 (kableExtra alone drags in a substantial
  subtree; rmarkdown/knitr pull the rendering deps). This is the two-layer model
  in action: DESCRIPTION declares intent (direct, by role); renv.lock captures 
  the full pinned reality.

