  It's generated from a template at zzc init

  The source is templates/DESCRIPTION, a template with ${VAR} placeholders:

  Package: ${PKG_NAME}
  Title: ${PKG_NAME} Data Analysis
  Version: 0.0.0.9000
  Authors@R:
      ${AUTHORS_R}
  Description: Data analysis workspace for the ${PKG_NAME} project.
  License: ${LICENSE_TYPE}
  Encoding: ${PKG_ENCODING}
  Roxygen: list(markdown = TRUE)
  RoxygenNote: ${ROXYGEN_VERSION}
  Suggests:
      tinytest (>= 1.4.0)

  At scaffold time, zzcollab runs envsubst (lib/templates.sh, the same
  substitution pass that handles $CONTAINER_RUNTIME, $UBUNTU_CODENAME, etc.) and
  fills in:
  - ${PKG_NAME} — from the project directory name (basename)
  - ${AUTHORS_R} — from your zzcollab config's author settings
  - ${LICENSE_TYPE}, ${PKG_ENCODING}, ${ROXYGEN_VERSION} — config/defaults

  What the seed looks like

  The first-time DESCRIPTION is minimal: metadata plus Suggests: tinytest (>= 
  1.4.0) (the ship-with test framework from the minimal bundle) and no Imports 
  at all. Everything after that — your actual Imports/Suggests — you accumulate
  as you write code, by hand or via renv::snapshot / zzrenvcheck.

  This ties back to the "no regenerate-DESCRIPTION flag" point: the template is
  a one-time seed, not a derivation. Regenerating it would reset to this stub
  and lose both your curated metadata and every dependency you've since declared
  — unlike renv.lock, which is fully derived and safe to reseed.

