⏺ Two things here — the actual render bug, and your architectural question,
  which is the more important one.

  The render bug is not kableExtra — it's TeX Live permissions

  The log settles it. The setup chunk (2/12 [setup], where library(kableExtra)
  lives) passed — the render got past it to the LaTeX stage. It then died on:

  open(>/usr/local/texlive/tlpkg/texlive.tlpdb) failed: Permission denied
  chmod(...) failed: Operation not permitted ...
  /opt/texlive/texmf-var/web2c/...

  Because options(tinytex.install_packages = TRUE), tinytex tried to tlmgr 
  update --self and rebuild formats at render time — but the non-root analyst
  user can't write /usr/local/texlive or /opt/texlive/texmf-var. My earlier
  chown fix covered /opt/renv but not the TeX tree. peng1 renders fine because
  its reports' LaTeX needs are fully covered by the pre-baked warm-up, so
  tinytex never tries a runtime install; fisher's reports (xelatex + extra
  packages) exceed the warm-up, trigger a runtime install, and hit the
  permission wall. So the lock and kableExtra are fine — this is a
  container-permissions issue.

  Your architectural question

  Yes — that block is exactly what bakes every renv.lock package into the image,
  and your instinct is legitimate. There are two established models:

  Bake (current). renv::restore() installs the whole closure into the image at
  build time. The image is the environment: self-contained, offline-capable,
  content-addressable, no runtime restore. Cost: the image is coupled to
  renv.lock — every package you add changes the lock, busts the RENV_LOCK_HASH
  cache layer, and forces a full image rebuild (the ~7-minute render builds
  you've been watching). That coupling is the thing bothering you.

  Engine-only (your instinct). The image provides just the R engine + system
  libs + LaTeX + dev tooling. Project packages live in renv.lock and are
  restored at runtime into a persistent library (a mounted ~/.cache/R/renv or a
  named volume, cached in CI). The image is stable — it rebuilds only when R,
  system deps, or LaTeX change, not when you add an analysis package. Cost: a
  first-run restore (slower once, then cached), and you must manage that
  persistent cache so it isn't re-restored every container start.

  Neither is wrong; they trade image-churn for runtime-restore complexity. For a
  research workflow where you add packages constantly, engine-only decouples
  the two and is arguably the better fit — which is essentially what zzcollab's
  "reusable team image" pattern already gestures at (build a stable base once,
  push to a registry, layer packages via renv).

  Where this leaves us

  The two issues are separable: the TeX-permission bug fails the render under
  either model (runtime tinytex install as non-root), so it needs fixing
  regardless. The bake-vs-engine-only choice is a larger design decision.

  My suggestion: fix the TeX permissions now to get fisher green (small,
  targeted — make the TeX tree writable, or pre-bake the full LaTeX set and turn
  off runtime install), and treat the engine-only refactor as a deliberate,
  separate change once both repos are green. But if you'd rather pivot the
  architecture first, I can lay out the engine-only Dockerfile + runtime-restore
  flow concretely.

  Which do you want — patch the TeX permissions to get green, or take up the
  engine-only model now?

