❯ what does ' honor LinkingTo' mean?                                            

⏺ zzrenvcheck v0.7.0 is committed and pushed (13df6ac) — task #4 done. Your
  question:

  What "honor LinkingTo" means

  LinkingTo is a DESCRIPTION field for packages whose C/C++ headers your 
  compiled code needs at build time. The canonical case is fisher's: it has C++
  in src/ that uses Rcpp, so DESCRIPTION says LinkingTo: Rcpp. That makes Rcpp's
  headers available when R compiles src/. It's different from Imports (R-level
  availability at runtime) and Depends (attached to the search path).

  The bug it fixes: zzrenvcheck finds dependencies by scanning R source for
  library(pkg), require(pkg), pkg::fn, and roxygen @import. But a LinkingTo
  package like Rcpp is used only at the C++ compilation level — your R code
  never writes library(Rcpp) or Rcpp::. (R/RcppExports.R calls .Call(...) with
  native symbols, not Rcpp::.) So the scanner never sees Rcpp in R code.

  The consequence was:
  - zzrenvcheck saw Rcpp declared in DESCRIPTION but "not used in code" →
  flagged it as unused.
  - Worse, sync/fresh would then remove Rcpp from DESCRIPTION and renv.lock —
  which breaks the package, since it can't compile without Rcpp.

  "Honoring LinkingTo" means teaching zzrenvcheck that packages in LinkingTo
  (and Depends) are genuinely used — structurally, via compiled linkage or
  attachment — even though they don't appear as library()/:: in R source.
  Concretely (the v0.7.0 change): the new parse_description_structural() reads
  LinkingTo + Depends, and those packages are excluded from the "unused" report
  and protected from removal in sync/fresh.

  So it's the exact fisher problem: without this, a make reset-lock (fresh) or a
  cleanup would have silently dropped Rcpp from fisher's manifest and broken
  the compiled Fisher-exact-test core. Now Rcpp stays put.

